{"t":1792144071,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"fb96cae8-2f9b-48e6-bfeb-047d9b7b987a","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-16T07:50:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 160750\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n350 AM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nThe Gale Watch was upgraded to a Gale Warning for late tonight\nthrough Saturday. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A strong cold front brings gusty winds, potential for \n severe weather, and below average temperatures.\n\n- 2) Dry conditions through next week with temperatures \n returning to mid-October normals.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong cold front brings gusty winds, potential \nfor severe weather, and below average temperatures.\n\nModel guidance is in relatively good agreement showing a potent \npositively tilted trough stretching from the great lakes down \ntowards the Great Plains tracking eastward this morning. At the \nsurface, the trough pushes a strong cold front across the forecast \narea this afternoon and into the evening. This morning, ahead of the \nfront, cloud cover decreases allowing for sunny conditions and warm \ntemperatures. Highs today rise into the 80s for most with higher \nelevations staying in the 70s. With warm conditions and sunny skies, \na line of thunderstorms is expected to develop along the \npassing cold front. The Storm Prediction Center has the north \neast portions of the forecast area spreading from the Washington\nDC metro area to central MD and eastward, in a slight risk for \nsevere weather today. The primary threat will be damaging wind \ngusts with NE MD in a 30% probability for severe wind. \nAdditionally, a quick QLCS tornado is possible, mainly in NE MD,\nwhere SPC has a 2% tornado risk. \n\nPrecipitation lingers behind the cold front as the upper trough \npivots overhead. Gusty winds remain possible beginning Friday night \nand persisting through Saturday. Winds will likely remain elevated, \nalthough higher elevations along the Alleghenies may have peak gusts \nbetween 30 and 40 mph. Areas are expected to remain below wind\nadvisory criteria although a few isolated gusts along the ridges\nare possible. \n\nNoticeably cooler temperatures rush in behind the front with \nhigh temperatures on Saturday being approximately 25-30 degrees \ncolder compared to Friday. Highs on Saturday will be in the 40s \nand 50s across the forecast area with overnight lows dropping \ninto the 30s to low 40s. Frost/Freeze headlines are likely for \nearly Sunday morning, mainly west of I-95 where winds will be \nlower.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions through next week with temperatures \nreturning to mid-October normals.\n\nHeading into next week, high pressure remains overhead through the \nmiddle of next week. A dry and benign weather pattern is expected \nduring this time frame with near-normal temperatures for mid-\nOctober. Although a few global ensemble members depict a coastal low \nforming off the Mid-Atlantic shore early next week, most members \nmaintain dry and seasonable weather. If this low pressure system \nwere to move closer to the coast, chances for precipitation \ncould change next week however confidence is low.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nMVFR to IFR ceilings continue through early this morning before\nconditions improve to VFR. Foggy conditions continue at CHO this\nmorning with patchy fog lingering across the other terminals. A\nstrong cold front crosses the terminals this afternoon and into\nthis evening, bringing a chance for thunderstorms, gusty winds\nand a wind shift. Sub-VFR conditions are possible during storms.\nSome storms may be severe with damaging winds are the primary\nthreat. Low clouds are possible again tonight with VFR\nconditions returning Saturday. For the winds, southwest winds\nthis morning shift to northwest in the wake of the cold front.\nWinds remain out of the northwest on Saturday, gusting 20 to 30\nknots. Winds diminish on Sunday with VFR conditions expected. \n\nVFR conditions are expected to persist for all terminals at the \nbeginning of next week. Light northeasterly wind on Monday shifts \nnortherly by Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSmall Craft Advisories are in effect beginning at 9AM this\nmorning across all waters with winds gusting 20 knots. A strong\ncold front crosses the waters this afternoon and into the\nevening, bringing gusty winds and a chance for thunderstorms.\nSMWs are possible as storms cross the waters later today. Winds\nshift to northwesterly behind the front tonight and increase to\ngusts around 34 knots. The Gale Watch has been upgraded to a\nGale Warning for late tonight through Saturday. Winds shift to\nnortheast on Sunday and are expected to remain below SCA\ncriteria. \n\nWind gusts remain below SCA criteria on Monday with light \nnortheasterly winds. Northerly winds could approach SCA criteria on \nTuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory from 9 AM this morning to 6 AM EDT \n     Saturday for MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 9 AM this morning to 6 AM EDT \n     Saturday for ANZ530>543.\n     Gale Warning from 6 AM Saturday to 6 AM EDT Sunday for \n     ANZ532>534-537-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...AVS/KVH\nAVIATION...AVS/KVH\nMARINE...AVS/KVH\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792144016,"days":{"2026-10-16":{"hi":83,"lo":50,"pop":77,"short":"Patchy Fog"},"2026-10-17":{"hi":55,"lo":39,"pop":39,"short":"Chance Rain Showers then Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-18":{"hi":57,"lo":42,"pop":8,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-19":{"hi":61,"lo":46,"pop":12,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-20":{"hi":66,"lo":49,"pop":10,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-21":{"hi":66,"lo":48,"pop":5,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-22":{"hi":64,"lo":null,"pop":7,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1792141200